@essay rh="FRONT MATTER · WHAT THIS VOLUME IS"
# What this volume is
drop: Volume One is the world: the scripture the Empire inherited, the history it rewrote, and the institutions, Dominions and Trials it built. This volume is the people who have to live inside all of that. It holds one dossier for every character with weight in the 39th Trial, from the woman at the kitchen table in Aurelia's outer districts to the man on the throne who taught himself never to be surprised.
A dossier here does what an entry in Volume One's people of the text does. It starts from what the files already say, keeps that separate from what's only inferred, and leaves most of the page ruled for you. The difference is that these people are yours. Nothing in a dossier outranks the manuscript, and where your files disagree with each other the page says so out loud instead of quietly picking a winner.
Every major character gets a faith and scripture spread. It covers what they believe and who taught it to them, what the Temple teaches about someone like them, the biblical name or story they echo and where the echo breaks, the verses they'd quote and the ones nobody has ever read to them, and the exact pages in Volume One to go back to. The novel's religious line runs between faith the Temple can use and faith that answers to something it doesn't control. Every person in this book stands somewhere on that line, and the faith spread is where you find out where.
pull: "Belief has a cost and a teacher." Rule IV, from Volume One.
side:
k: What it isn't
stext: It won't replace a cast list or a plot summary, and it won't answer anything for you. Where the files are silent the page is ruled, and the ruled space outranks anything printed near it.
k: Volume One pages
stext: Cross-references give the folio in Volume One, so "Vol. I, {v1:hannah}" means page {v1:hannah} of The World Dossier, where Hannah, Abigail and Michal are.
marg
see: Volume One, the people of the text, page 369.
/end

@essay id="f2-how" rh="FRONT MATTER · HOW A DOSSIER WORKS"
# How a dossier works
drop: Every dossier opens with a portrait page, a frame for a drawing, a reference image or Canva art, and the person's record underneath it. After that the pages run in the same order for everyone at the same length, so you can read across the cast and see where the Empire holds and where it's already coming apart.
k: The full dossier, about sixteen pages
1. The portrait and the record.
2. The Registry card and every name they answer to.
3. The years, set against the Empire's calendar.
4. Standing, the clerk's version and the true one.
5. Faith and scripture, what they believe and who taught it.
6. Faith and scripture, the text: the name, the verses, Volume One.
7. Body and fear, belief arc, power and people, voice and contradiction.
8. The working pages: the machinery around them, one excavated scene, one ordinary hour, one relationship from both sides, and a scene lab.
k: The shorter dossiers
Thomas, Zion, Solomon, the House Aurem staff, Senior Minister Bell and the Prelate get about seven pages each, with the faith spread pressed onto a single page. Each Covenant Daughter gets about eight, with a page tracking her through the three phases.
side:
k: The one rule
stext: No character gets a belief they've never paid for. If the creed page names a conviction, the contradiction page has to name what it cost.
sbox: Photocopy first | The working pages expect a second and third draft. Copy them before you fill them.
marg
see: The labels, on the next page.
/end

@page rh="FRONT MATTER · LABELS AND SOURCES" m="VOL. I · {v1:reading}–{v1:labels}"
# Labels and sources
The page signals are the ones in Volume One. Quoted scripture sits behind a fine rule with its reference underneath. The heavy frame with the pink edge is always the Empire's reading and never the text's. A broken frame marks a real dispute among serious readers. Ruled space is yours.
tabrl:
| Label | What it means here |
| (no label) | Your files say it, with the source tag in parentheses. Scripture and history with a reference are research, as in Volume One. |
| INTERPRETATION | A reading of a character or a passage, offered as a reading |
| CONTESTED | A real dispute about scripture among scholars or traditions |
| INFERENCE | A conclusion drawn from your files that they don't state |
| POSSIBLE | An idea worth considering, and not canon |
| OPEN QUESTION | Something only you can decide |
| CONFLICT | Two of your files disagree |
| SUPERSEDED | Older material that no longer controls |
| DECIDED | A call you've made. Canon from here on. |
tabrl:
| Tag | Source |
| FR 0–44 | Final Rulings. These outrank everything. |
| TL · TR · DOM · MEN · CR | Timeline · Trials · Dominions · Men in the Empire · The Crownward |
| DR3 · WR · AM | Draft Three (chapter one) · Writing Rules · the old Companion |
| Ruling | A call you've made in conversation, logged in the back of this volume |

